course: dictionary
lesson: 10414
title: One word, many meanings: trim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Trim can mean cut a little off, a short haircut, a strip of decoration, decorate, or neat and slim.
explain: «Hooves keep growing and need trimming every few weeks.» Here trim is a verb. Swap in "cutting a little off" and the sentence still makes sense, so that is the meaning.
explain: «The brand-new caravan is canary-yellow with green trim.» Here trim is a noun. The words canary-yellow and green show it is a colour on the edges, so it is decoration, not cutting.
fact: Trim is a word with several meanings. Read the sentence to find which one fits.
fact: As a verb, trim means to cut a little off, as in «trim the hedge» or «trim your nails». Its forms are trims, trimmed and trimming.
fact: As a noun, a trim can be a short haircut that just tidies the hair, or a strip of material used to decorate edges.
fact: As a verb, trim can also mean to decorate, as in «trim the Christmas tree». As an adjective, it means neat and slim.
fact: «Trimmings» are extra bits, like decorations or side dishes.
exercise: «Zoo elephants are trained to lift their feet so keepers can trim their nails.» What does trim mean here? | Hide them under a blanket | *Cut a little off to keep them neat | Paint them in bright colours | Make them grow longer
exercise: «The caravan has green trim along its edges.» What is trim here? | *A strip of decoration along the edges | Neat and slim | A short haircut | To cut a little off
exercise: Dad says, «I need a trim before the wedding.» Which meaning fits best? | Neat and slim | *A short haircut that tidies the hair | To decorate a tree | To cut a hedge
test: «Please trim the edges of the picture so it fits the frame.» What does trim mean? | *To cut a little off | A short haircut | Very loud | A strip of lace
test: «We will trim the Christmas tree tonight.» What does trim mean here? | To cut a little off | Thin and neat | A haircut | *To decorate
exam: «She wore a slim, trim black suit.» What does trim mean here? | *Neat and slim | A strip of decoration | A short haircut | To cut a little off
